{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Mateo, the pertinent information is: sensory: dual access needs; mobility: cane and orientation support; medical: none affecting school; and behaviour: may withdraw when access breaks down. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: incidental learning from distant events; fast group discussion; unannounced room changes; and materials that are only visual or auditory. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Mateo, the most useful EA actions are to ensure communication access before instruction; describe who, where, and what is happening; preserve independent travel opportunities; and coordinate with intervener and specialists. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-B-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.4625, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.475, "end": 1.75, "start_char": 22, "end_char":